          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good score on the Likeable Person Test?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Any score above 70% is considered good. Scores between 70–79% indicate solid likeability with some specific areas to develop. Scores of 80% and above reflect strong social habits that most people around you will notice and appreciate. Rather than chasing a specific number, focus on your lowest-scoring category — that's where the most useful growth is."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a low Likeable Person Test score mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A low score means certain social habits are creating friction in how others experience you — not that you're fundamentally unlikeable. Research on the 'liking gap' (Boothby et al., 2018) shows that people consistently underestimate how much others like them. If you scored low, look at which specific categories dragged the overall score down. Those are the areas to focus on, and they're all learnable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Likeable Person Test category matters most?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Empathy and Communication tend to have the widest downstream impact because they affect nearly every social interaction you have. However, if your Trustworthiness or Reliability score is particularly low, those are worth prioritising first — damaged trust takes longer to repair than improving communication style."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I retake the Likeable Person Test to track improvement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and it's a good idea. Take the test, work on your lowest category for 30 days with one specific behaviour change, then retake it. Most people see a measurable shift in multiple categories after a focused month — partly because the nine dimensions are interconnected. Improving your listening habits, for instance, tends to lift your Empathy, Communication, and Social Skills scores simultaneously."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why did I score lower than I expected on the Likeable Person Test?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Two common reasons. First, you may have answered as your aspirational self rather than your actual self — how you behave on your best day rather than your average Tuesday. Second, the 'liking gap' research by Boothby et al. (2018) consistently shows that anxious or self-critical people rate themselves as less likeable than they actually are. If your score felt harsh, it's worth asking whether you were honest or simply pessimistic."
          }
        }
      ]
